  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a Venus flytrap know an insect is there?",
      "a": "Each lobe has a few trigger hairs. Touching one sends an electrical signal, and the trap closes only if hairs are touched twice within about 20 seconds, which means something alive is moving inside.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the trigger hairs?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the trap close so fast?",
      "a": "The lobes are curved and stored under tension. A rapid shift of water in the cells flips the curve the other way, like popping a bent lid, so the trap snaps shut in about a tenth of a second.",
      "asks": [
        "How fast does a flytrap close?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a Venus flytrap digest insects?",
      "a": "Further touches tell the trap to seal itself into a stomach and pour out digestive juices. Over about five to twelve days it soaks up the nutrients, then reopens, leaving the dry husk behind.",
      "asks": [
        "How long does a flytrap take to eat a fly?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a Venus flytrap eat insects?",
      "a": "It grows in wet, boggy soil with very little nitrogen. Insects supply the nitrogen and other nutrients it cannot get from the ground, though it still makes its sugar by photosynthesis.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are some plants carnivorous?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where do Venus flytraps grow in the wild?",
      "a": "Only in a small area of boggy land in North and South Carolina in the United States. They are rare in the wild and protected by law.",
      "asks": [
        "Where do flytraps come from?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
